SoX 14.4.2's `silence` effect with -l never resets its pause count when sound
resumes: every pause in an utterance adds to one total, and the effect stops
once the pauses together reach the end duration. conch's capture recipe uses -l
(so trailing words stay), and this cut dictation every few seconds of natural
speech. Measured: 1 s tone / 1 s silence x10 with `silence -l 1 0.15 2% 1 3.5 2%`
stopped after 7.6 s unpatched; patched, it runs to 22.5 s (19 s + the 3.5 s
it keeps), as it does without -l. Apply in the source tree with `patch -p1`.

--- a/src/silence.c
+++ b/src/silence.c
@@ -497,6 +497,17 @@
                     /* Case 1b */
                     else if (threshold)
                     {
+                        /* With leave_silence the quiet samples were already
+                         * copied out, so Case 1a never flushes and the hold-off
+                         * count was never reset: every pause in an utterance
+                         * added to one total, and the effect stopped when the
+                         * pauses together reached the duration. Sound has
+                         * resumed, so this pause is over. */
+                        if (silence->leave_silence)
+                        {
+                            silence->stop_holdoff_offset = 0;
+                            silence->stop_holdoff_end = 0;
+                        }
                         /* Not holding off so copy into output buffer */
                         for (j = 0; j < effp->in_signal.channels; j++)
                         {
